WEBVTT

00:00:00.028 --> 00:00:07.462
Three-valued logic becomes easier when we reason through a few combinations instead of trying to memorize a

00:00:07.462 --> 00:00:09.717
large truth table. Take TRUE AND UNKNOWN.

00:00:09.717 --> 00:00:12.527
The final result is UNKNOWN.

00:00:12.527 --> 00:00:17.257
We know one side is true, but the unknown side could still be false,

00:00:17.257 --> 00:00:20.642
so the whole expression cannot be guaranteed true.

00:00:20.642 --> 00:00:23.062
Now take FALSE AND UNKNOWN.

00:00:23.062 --> 00:00:25.597
That result is definitely FALSE.

00:00:25.597 --> 00:00:31.252
Once one side of an AND condition is false, the whole expression cannot become true regardless of the missing

00:00:31.252 --> 00:00:37.452
side. For OR, TRUE OR UNKNOWN is definitely TRUE because one true condition is enough.

00:00:37.452 --> 00:00:45.492
FALSE OR UNKNOWN remains UNKNOWN because the missing side could still turn the expression into true.

00:00:45.492 --> 00:00:46.872
And NOT UNKNOWN remains UNKNOWN.

00:00:46.872 --> 00:00:50.052
Here is a practical way to think about it.

00:00:50.052 --> 00:00:56.982
Ask whether the known information is already enough to determine the outcome.

00:00:56.982 --> 00:01:03.072
If an online order is already marked fraudulent, then “fraudulent OR missing address verification” is still

00:01:03.072 --> 00:01:08.002
true. We do not need the second condition to know the answer.

00:01:08.002 --> 00:01:12.817
If a patient is definitely inactive, then “active AND unknown consent” is definitely false.

00:01:12.817 --> 00:01:17.612
The missing consent cannot rescue the rule.

00:01:17.612 --> 00:01:23.702
When you run the truth-table example, focus less on memorizing every cell and more on asking:

00:01:23.702 --> 00:01:26.737
is the known side enough to settle the decision?

00:01:26.737 --> 00:01:30.563
That reasoning scales better when conditions become more complex.
